WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.959 --> 00:00:05.338
Classic car ownership is one
of these things that is not just personal.

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:08.341
It is actually incredibly extroverted.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.511
When you own a classic car,
you can own it for your own

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:15.181
personal reasons
and how you feel in that vehicle.

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:19.644
But countless times you're driving
one of these cars, you pull up to a stop

00:00:19.644 --> 00:00:24.607
sign, somebody shouts, "I had one of these
back in 1973 and I loved it so much".

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:30.697
So there's this huge spike in the classic
car market as a result of that,

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:34.993
a lot more people are turning
their classic cars into electric vehicles.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:39.039
These are fickle, finicky vehicles.

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:43.626
But when it's run by a massive battery
and electric motor, it's

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:46.296
basically as reliable as a microwave oven.

00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:49.632
To do it elegantly is really an art.

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:54.137
It's really about the love
and the appreciation of this artform

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:59.017
and the ability to own it
and drive it as trouble-free

00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:02.479
and as seamlessly as possible.
That's what we're about.

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:13.740
I'm Mark Davis.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.744
I'm the founder of Moment
Motor Company here in Austin, Texas.

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:22.332
And what we do is we transform
vintage cars into electric vehicles.

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:28.088
I've been obsessed with cars since birth.

00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:32.592
I love them in a way that seems unnatural.

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:38.681
You know, at this point,
we've done over 20 different chassis,

00:01:38.681 --> 00:01:45.355
and we've applied our technology to lots
and lots of different types of vehicles.

00:01:45.563 --> 00:01:49.651
Front wheel drive, rear wheel drive,
all wheel drive, trucks,

00:01:50.068 --> 00:01:52.862
sports cars, sedans, you name it.

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:58.785
Classic car ownership
for as much enjoyment

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:04.165
as you get from driving the car,
there is this constant dread

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:06.793
that you have,
you know, under the surface,

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:10.213
thinking about what's likely to go wrong
as you're driving that car.

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:13.716
You can smell things.
You might hear something.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:18.304
And it just is an endless
kind of undercurrent of worry.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.765
We have owners that come to us.

00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:26.229
They want the seamlessness of
just not worrying and just driving.

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:30.733
That's what the electrification does.

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:35.113
We have other clients who come to us

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:39.701
and they want the ability
to appreciate that art form,

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:40.869
but they want to do it

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:44.706
with a sense that they're doing something
with less impact to our planet.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.714
So the first step, when a
client comes to us with a particular car,

00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:57.010
really what we're trying to do is assure
that this is going to be feasible.

00:02:57.051 --> 00:03:00.138
I don't want to be responsible
for tearing down a client car

00:03:00.138 --> 00:03:02.849
and finding out that we really just don't
have a good solution.

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:07.687
We really like to go
and look at every aspect of that vehicle.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:13.526
Really, what we can do is start looking
at the components that we know work,

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:17.822
blocking out a solution in our minds
that we think will be

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.824
a reasonable approach to electrifying.

00:03:19.824 --> 00:03:21.826
"So here's what we got back
here, huh?" Once

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:24.913
we've got a plan,
we start tearing it apart.

00:03:27.457 --> 00:03:30.251
Really, all we are doing
is we're pulling out the combustion

00:03:30.251 --> 00:03:34.214
drive train: engine, transmission,
fuel system, things like that.

00:03:34.505 --> 00:03:38.635
We're doing that
as carefully and meticulously as possible.

00:03:42.305 --> 00:03:44.307
Our goal is to not hack this up.

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:45.934
Our motto is "First, do no harm.", right?

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:49.896
And you really don't want to dive
into one of these beautiful classics

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:54.776
with a grinder and a cut off wheel
and slice it all up and do

00:03:54.776 --> 00:03:56.152
horrible things to it.

00:03:56.152 --> 00:03:59.322
The next step...
that starts with a 3D scan.

00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:03.826
The first step in that is to put down
locating dots all over,

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:07.747
so the scanner can know
exactly where it is in 3D space.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:15.255
The next step is to get the engine bay,
the trunk, the underbody scanned

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:20.385
so that we can bring it into our CAD
application and start designing the parts

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:24.222
that we need to install the drive train.

00:04:30.186 --> 00:04:34.482
Once we have the car scanned,
we're able to take these battery boxes,

00:04:34.732 --> 00:04:39.404
motors, and our motor mounting systems and drop them into the car virtually.

00:04:39.404 --> 00:04:46.077
Then, we go through the painstaking
task of custom designing and engineering

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.579
he brackets, the platform,

00:04:48.579 --> 00:04:53.501
the mounts that will put these components
into that specific chassis.

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:58.506
These are not modern
electric vehicles with a skateboard

00:04:58.756 --> 00:05:00.466
of batteries underneath them.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.385
And so we don't have the luxury

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:06.222
of tearing the floor out and replacing it with a big battery pack.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:09.517
We have to put them in the places
in the car that that are now empty,

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:11.602
which are typically
the engine bay and the trunk.

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:16.566
We send those designs out to the laser
cutters,

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:19.861
the water jetters, the machine shops,
who will then

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.697
send us the results and we'll be able to

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:26.701
bring them back into the shop and test
fit them on the car.

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:33.666
Very quickly we arrive at a final design
and a final set of components that we can

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:38.254
weld, finish, send off
for powder coat and paint,

00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:40.965
and then it will come back to us
for installation.

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:48.639
So in 2021 in the US, at auction -
that's online

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:53.519
and in person auctions, 36,000
classic cars were bought in the U.S.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:59.150
and that's a huge increase even over,
you know, pretty high pre-pandemic levels.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:04.364
So it's about 56% increase over 2019
and that represents

00:06:04.364 --> 00:06:09.160
2.2 billion in transactions
and that's an even larger increase.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.079
So these cars
are going for even more money

00:06:12.246 --> 00:06:13.456
That's just auctions.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:16.542
The peer to peer market,
which people selling their cars

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:21.714
to each other on Craigslist, Facebook,
any number of platforms like that is about

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:22.673
ten times bigger.

00:06:22.673 --> 00:06:28.596
So it's, it's really a vital and liquid
market and it's booming at the moment.

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:32.266
Electrifying classic cars...
it is a controversial thing.

00:06:32.266 --> 00:06:36.687
I mean, the people that do
this are told all the time that they are

00:06:36.687 --> 00:06:41.067
"taking the soul out of a car",
and that the sound and the smell

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:43.361
is a huge part of the experience.

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:46.531
And there may be some truth to that
for some people.

00:06:47.281 --> 00:06:49.325
But if they're not getting out
to drive the car

00:06:50.201 --> 00:06:52.245
because they're not sure it'll start,

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:54.664
that's valid
on the other side of the argument, too.

00:06:54.664 --> 00:06:56.499
It's an act of preservation.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:58.418
It started

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:03.214
about a decade ago just with people who decided to electrify

00:07:03.256 --> 00:07:07.552
their cherished classic, and some of those hobby projects turned into businesses.

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:11.973
It's a really interesting skunkworks
required

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:14.350
to kind of make one of these shops work,

00:07:14.684 --> 00:07:17.103
and that's one of the reasons why there's not a ton of them.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:24.235
I like to describe myself as a wayward engineer.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.781
I went to engineering school
and went to work in the software industry

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:31.451
and spent a long time in the technology
industry.

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:36.998
The whole way, though, I did what
we were told to do, which is do the thing

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:40.960
that you're good at to make the money
so that you can do the thing that you love

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:44.213
on the weekends and on the side.

00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:46.299
And I did that and I followed the money,

00:07:47.008 --> 00:07:50.303
and I finally just couldn't
take it anymore.

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:53.931
I was sick of hot
rodding my cars in my garage

00:07:54.098 --> 00:07:58.186
in a couple of hours in the evening
after my kids went to bed.

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:01.272
I really wanted to do something
that I loved, something I

00:08:01.272 --> 00:08:02.190
could be passionate about.

00:08:03.274 --> 00:08:05.568
I think the opportunity of electrifying

00:08:05.568 --> 00:08:10.239
classic cars kind of presented itself
as a unique combination

00:08:10.281 --> 00:08:15.286
of the things that I was good at
and the things that I loved.

00:08:15.578 --> 00:08:19.874
I could apply technology to something
that was not just a piece of software

00:08:19.874 --> 00:08:22.001
for a customer.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:25.379
So that opportunity was something that I took.

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:30.801
I made the leap and I started the company and here we are.

00:08:30.801 --> 00:08:36.432
Once we've designed and test fit all of these brackets and components,

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:37.767
then the process

00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:41.896
is really taking these finished parts
and installing everything in the car.

00:08:43.272 --> 00:08:47.068
If you look at an electric motor,
it's typically a fraction of the size

00:08:47.151 --> 00:08:48.277
of the combustion engine.

00:08:48.277 --> 00:08:53.741
And so oftentimes we can fit the motor
and a single speed reduction gearbox

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:57.036
in the same space
where the original transmission was,

00:08:57.286 --> 00:08:59.956
and the entire engine
bay becomes available

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:02.291
for us to put batteries
and other components in it.

00:09:04.961 --> 00:09:07.171
We're also looking to make sure
that we're not putting

00:09:07.338 --> 00:09:11.175
stresses on the chassis in areas
that it wasn't designed to take.

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:13.719
If you just put the electric motor

00:09:13.719 --> 00:09:16.180
in the engine bay
and you put batteries in the trunk, you're

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:20.434
going to end up an incredibly unbalanced
and possibly dangerous car.

00:09:21.435 --> 00:09:26.732
So we try to distribute the weight
of the batteries throughout the chassis.

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:32.113
Our goal is to retain the performance
and the feel of this car.

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:37.743
These cars were designed 50 years ago
very precisely by hundreds,

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:42.164
if not thousands of engineers
who were trying to create a certain drive.

00:09:42.164 --> 00:09:45.710
We want to respect that
as much as possible.

00:09:46.419 --> 00:09:49.880
Then, we are wiring everything together.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.634
Wiring up a car... it's kind of in two phases.

00:09:53.843 --> 00:09:57.888
There is the high voltage wiring
that powers the motor

00:09:58.306 --> 00:10:00.558
and connects the battery pack to the system.

00:10:00.558 --> 00:10:02.852
And that is relatively simple and straightforward.

00:10:03.269 --> 00:10:04.186
But there's another

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:08.232
set of wiring that happens in these cars
that we call vehicle control wiring.

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:13.863
We want this car to be driven and enjoyed the same way that it's always been

00:10:13.988 --> 00:10:18.159
with the beautiful gauges that were designed in the sixties with the

00:10:18.868 --> 00:10:22.330
analog sweeping needles and things like that.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:26.876
Nobody's buying this wanting a Tesla, right?

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:31.631
They don't want to get into a classic car
and have a giant

00:10:31.631 --> 00:10:34.425
iPad on their dashboard.

00:10:38.262 --> 00:10:39.305
There's so many components

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:42.308
that we install, so many different things
that have to come together,

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:45.311
and it's kind of like we've put an orchestra together for the first time

00:10:46.187 --> 00:10:49.649
and those first drives, when you get in the car,

00:10:51.317 --> 00:10:56.405
tap your little conductor's wand
on the thing and say, Okay, let's do this.

00:10:56.405 --> 00:10:59.408
Let's try this piece from the start.

00:11:04.163 --> 00:11:06.666
You go out on the drive and you know what

00:11:06.707 --> 00:11:09.543
the drummer is, you know, pushing,
pushing a little too hard and

00:11:10.878 --> 00:11:13.756
you know, the trumpets are just,
just not quite hitting that high note.

00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:17.426
You hear all of those things,
you take it all in and you think, Okay,

00:11:17.843 --> 00:11:18.719
there's something here.

00:11:18.719 --> 00:11:21.097
We're going to be able to get there,
but we got to work on these things.

00:11:21.889 --> 00:11:25.810
We are out there putting hundreds,
if not thousands of miles on the car,

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:27.728
making sure that it responds

00:11:27.728 --> 00:11:32.775
the way that it should, intuitively drives
the way you would expect it to.

00:11:32.817 --> 00:11:37.488
We're able to manipulate torque curves
and all sorts of inputs and controls.

00:11:37.488 --> 00:11:42.910
We can change the way that the motor
reacts to the throttle position,

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:43.536
if you will.

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:48.833
We take this time
to tune each section of this orchestra

00:11:48.833 --> 00:11:52.336
and bring it all together
so that when you get in and you drive it,

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:59.510
it is a flawless performance,
one that makes people smile and cry

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:05.683
and engages them in a way that they have never driven before.

00:12:05.683 --> 00:12:07.852
And that's the goal.

00:12:08.185 --> 00:12:10.062
We often get the question, Well,
how much does this cost?

00:12:10.062 --> 00:12:11.439
And the reality is

00:12:11.439 --> 00:12:13.941
it depends a lot on the car
that we're that we're starting with:

00:12:14.608 --> 00:12:16.819
the size of the car,
the condition of the car

00:12:16.902 --> 00:12:19.739
and the performance
and range requirements of the owner.

00:12:19.739 --> 00:12:24.577
So it is a pretty large range,
but it can be anywhere from $50,000

00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:28.289
to $150,000.

00:12:30.916 --> 00:12:34.003
In terms of moving the needle on climate change,

00:12:34.003 --> 00:12:38.424
transportation is critical
and arguably the lowest hanging fruit.

00:12:38.841 --> 00:12:43.262
This is a huge swath of greenhouse
gas emissions and electrifying

00:12:43.262 --> 00:12:47.016
these vehicles is kind
of one of the easiest and fastest ways

00:12:48.184 --> 00:12:48.976
The new E.V.

00:12:48.976 --> 00:12:53.230
market last year in the world
was about 7 million vehicles.

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:56.484
That's about 10% of the global sales.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:58.694
Both globally and in the U.S.

00:12:58.694 --> 00:13:01.489
the E.V. market more than doubled
last year.

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:05.993
The adoption curve is steepening
quite a bit faster

00:13:06.076 --> 00:13:08.120
than most people even expected.

00:13:08.120 --> 00:13:11.916
By most people,
I would include auto executives in that.

00:13:11.999 --> 00:13:15.961
Ford Motor, for example,
is rushing to make electric vehicles

00:13:15.961 --> 00:13:19.632
as fast as they can. "Mr.
President...." "...This sucker's quick."

00:13:21.008 --> 00:13:22.009
There will always be

00:13:22.009 --> 00:13:26.847
people driving their gasoline cars,
even 50 years from now.

00:13:27.014 --> 00:13:30.059
Maybe more at that point.
And it'll be such a novelty.

00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:33.312
But I think the shift is going to happen
quickly.

00:13:33.354 --> 00:13:36.148
The adoption is going to be fast.

00:13:36.148 --> 00:13:38.567
And faster than we realized.

00:13:38.567 --> 00:13:42.905
The implications of maintenance
on a classic car with a 60 year

00:13:42.905 --> 00:13:45.908
old drive train are significant, right?

00:13:45.908 --> 00:13:49.662
It's a diminishing population of people
who are able to do that,

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:52.790
and the parts are hard to get
and the knowledge is hard to come by.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:58.838
And there's something that feels
a little bit like that's coming to an end.

00:13:58.838 --> 00:13:59.588
And I think

00:13:59.588 --> 00:14:04.176
that for people to put in place
a, you know, modern electric drive

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:06.220
train gives them a confidence in the future,

00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:09.890
gives them the, you know,
the feeling that they can no longer,

00:14:10.266 --> 00:14:14.395
you know, worry about that.

00:14:14.395 --> 00:14:20.818
When you go and you look at the beauty
and the simplicity

00:14:20.818 --> 00:14:25.155
involved in an electric motor,
I mean, there's only two parts where

00:14:25.698 --> 00:14:28.742
the moving parts touch one
another - on the bearings, right?

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:29.660
And that's it.

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:32.746
And everything
just spins with electromagnetism.

00:14:32.746 --> 00:14:34.832
There's no real comparison

00:14:34.832 --> 00:14:40.504
between that technology
and what we used to use to propel cars.

00:14:40.504 --> 00:14:42.882
So, I don't know,
I think that that is the future.

